1. POJO (Plain Old Java Object):
A POJO is a simple Java object that doesn’t have any special restrictions. It is a Java class with fields (attributes), constructors, getters, and setters to encapsulate the data. In your code, Pojo_Add_Place and Pojo_Location are examples of POJOs. These classes are used to represent the data structure that you want to work with in your tests.

Pojo_Add_Place: Represents the data related to a place (like its name, address, types, location, etc.).

Pojo_Location: Represents the location of the place with latitude (lat) and longitude (lng).

Example: Pojo_Add_Place:

public class Pojo_Add_Place {
    private int accuracy;
    private Pojo_Location location;
    private String name;
    private String phone_number;
    private String address;
    private List<String> types;
    private String website;
    private String language;

    // Getters and setters
}
This class is used to store the details about a place. When you send a request to an API, you’ll use this class to store the place details and then serialize it into JSON.

2. Serialization:
Serialization is the process of converting a POJO object into a format (like JSON) that can be sent over the network, typically in HTTP requests.

In your code:

You create a POJO object (Pojo_Add_Place).

The object contains all the details about a place.

Before sending this object to the API, you need to serialize it into JSON, which is a standard format that the server can understand.

How serialization works in your code:
In the method addPlacePayload from the Pojo_TestDataBuild class, you're creating a Pojo_Add_Place object and setting values for different fields (like accuracy, name, phone_number, etc.). After this, you pass the object to the API request body to send it to the server.

Serialization happens automatically when you pass the Pojo_Add_Place object as the request body.

The given().spec(requestSpecification()).body(data.addPlacePayload(name, language, address)) part of the code is where the Pojo_Add_Place object is serialized into JSON and sent to the server.

For instance:


Pojo_Add_Place p = new Pojo_Add_Place();
p.setAccuracy(50);
p.setName(name);
p.setPhone_number("(+91) 983 893 3937");
p.setAddress(address);
p.setWebsite("http://google.com");
p.setLanguage(language);

// Convert POJO into JSON for API request
res = given().spec(requestSpecification()).body(p);
At this point, the Pojo_Add_Place object p will be serialized into JSON when sent as the body of the HTTP request.

3. Deserialization:
Deserialization is the reverse of serialization. It’s the process of converting JSON data (usually in the response from the server) back into a POJO object, so you can access the values in your program.

When you send a request (like a GET request), the server responds with JSON data. You need to deserialize this JSON data into a Java object (like Pojo_Add_Place or Pojo_Location) so that you can work with it in your code.

How deserialization works in your code:
After the request is made and a response is received from the server, the response is in JSON format.

You use methods like getJsonPath() to extract values from the JSON response.

Deserialization happens when you convert the JSON response into Java objects.

For example, in the method verify_place_id_created_maps_to_using_get_place_api, you retrieve the place’s place_id from the response, and then you deserialize the JSON response to get other details about the place.


place_id = getJsonPath(response, "place_id"); // Extract place_id from response JSON
res = given().spec(requestSpecification()).queryParam("place_id", place_id);
user_calls_with_http_request(resource, "GET"); // Make GET request to fetch place details

// Get the name of the place from the response JSON
String actualName = getJsonPath(response, "name");
assertEquals(actualName, expectedName); // Compare the name in response with the expected value
Here’s how deserialization happens:

The JSON response from the API might look like this:

{
  "place_id": "12345",
  "name": "Test Place",
  "address": "123 Test St",
  "phone_number": "(+91) 983 893 3937"
}
The getJsonPath(response, "name") method will extract the value of the name field from the JSON response. This deserialization process allows you to interact with the JSON as if it were a regular Java object.

Code Relation of POJO, Serialization, and Deserialization:

POJO (Plain Old Java Object): Represents the structure of the data you want to work with. For example, Pojo_Add_Place represents the details of a place.

Serialization: When you want to send a request (like POST) to the API, you serialize the POJO object into JSON so that it can be transmitted over HTTP.

In your code, given().spec(requestSpecification()).body(data.addPlacePayload(name, language, address)) is where the Pojo_Add_Place object is serialized into JSON.

Deserialization: When you receive a response (like GET), you deserialize the JSON response into a POJO object so that you can easily access and work with the data.

In your code, getJsonPath(response, "name") extracts values from the JSON response, which is essentially deserialization.

Flow of Data:
Creating a POJO object: You create a Pojo_Add_Place object and fill it with data (name, address, etc.).

Serialization: The object is automatically converted into JSON when you send it in the request body (body(p)).

Server Response (JSON): The server sends back a response in JSON format (for example, the details of the place).

Deserialization: You extract values from the JSON response and convert them back into usable data in your code (e.g., place name, place ID).

In summary:

POJOs represent the data structure.

Serialization converts the POJO into JSON to send to the server.

Deserialization converts the JSON response back into a POJO or usable data in your code.

This is how POJO, serialization, and deserialization are tied together in your test automation code to interact with APIs.



